<title>USA: Hot and humid in Florida citrus groves - FASS.</title>
<headline>Hot and humid in Florida citrus groves - FASS.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-06-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>This week was generally hot and humid in Florida's citrus belt, with periodic rains and thunderstorms, the Florida Agricultural Statistics Service (FASS), said.</p>
<p>In its weekly weather and crop update, FASS said new growth is showing on trees of all ages and new crop fruit is making good progress.</p>
<p>Moisture is a little less than adequate according to FASS, and caretakers have reduced their irrigation.  Replanting is going on in the larger groves where older dead trees have been removed.  Summer herbicides are being applied between the rains.</p>
<p>FASS reported an estimated 6,353,000 boxes of Valencia oranges were harvested in the week ended May 26, up from 6,073,000 in the week ended May 18.</p>
<p>-- New York Commodity Desk, 212-859-1640</p>
